A||||6||Pretender||long answers required, about torque converters.
Z||000000||Pretender||07-03-99||11:23 AM||||will someone/'s explain what the purpose of the torque converter is, and why it would be better to have a different stall speed. What is the stall speed anyway?
Z||000001||batboy||07-03-99||12:38 PM||||I'm not an automotive engineer, but basically a torque convertor (T/C) allows a certain amount of slippage, especially when you take off from a dead stop to keep the engine from stalling out. You know what happens if you pop the clutch too fast on a manual transmission equipped car. If you have a stock or a mildly modified street engine, don't mess with a higher stall speed T/C because you don't need it. High performance street and racing engines with higher duration camshafts need a T/C with more stall speed because they bog out at very low rpm. Basically the rumpity-rump cams move the torque and horsepower curve higher up into the rpm range. All the major cam makers will recommend what stall speed works the best with their cams. I don't remember what stall speed most stock T/C are, but lets just say it's 1200 rpm. Leaving a stop light, you must rev the engine to about 1200 rpm before the T/C kicks in good. With an aftermarket T/C of 2000 rpm stall speed, you must rev the engine to 2000 rpm before it kicks in good. This puts you back up into the power range if you have a modified engine with a big cam.
Z||000002||Racer 1||07-04-99||12:45 AM||||A torque converter is only on a automatic transmission.It goes between your rear output shaft on you engine  and your input shaft on you auto transmission.Its like a fluid clutch it lets you car idle when you stop with it in drive position.A higher stall speed is not recommended for the fiero...
Z||000003||Ray Heath||07-06-99||04:35 PM||||If you modify the engine with some performance goodys, such as cam, pistons, etc., why would you not want a higher stall converter? We all know that the Fiero is quick on take-off with very little wheel spin, I would think the higher stall would make it quicker. I have a 2780 RPM stall converter I am putting in my 3.4 setup. 
Z||000004||Cooter||07-06-99||04:53 PM||||Ray- with a stall speed that high, you might want to add a very large trans fluid cooler. In normal driving you will not be at a high enough RPM to fully engage the converter, unless you have swapped the ring and pinion to something in the 5.38 to 1 range. This high stall will lead to a lot of heat build up, so a temp gauge would also be a wise investment. THe trans temp on my Suburban usually runs about 190-210 and that is with a large cooler and a stock converter. Any higher temp will shorten the life of the trans.
Z||000005||batboy||07-06-99||05:40 PM||||With that high of a stall speed, your car will be a pain to drive in stop and go driving. What size cam are you running? Chances are, you could use a much lower stall speed than that. Those types of torque convertors are for racing, not street driving.
Z||000006||theogre||07-06-99||06:17 PM||||cooter, don't forget, the ECM will lock the TCC at any even speed above around 30-35MPH. At that point the T/C is a solid unit and generates little heat.<p>In heavy traffic you are right tho that the TCC won't be locked, and the T/C will generate more heat, but it does that stock too. The bigger problem is that the higher stall T/C will not operate correctly in that type driving.<p>I would stringly recomend reinstalling the stock T/C, or just a better made one with the same stall. Only track raced cars get long term benifit from higher stall.<p>Stall speed is the point at which the T/C behaves as a solid unit, I.E. a locked clutch. There are a large number of things that effect it. Always try the stock unit first. Then if need be spend the rather stiff price for the performance units. The TH125 stock T/C is allot better than allot of people think if it's in good shape, and in a good tranny.
